y slowed uh slowed progress  um obviously a very big exception to  this has been the world of bits  um computers internet mobile internet  software  um and so silicon valley has somehow  been this uh been this dramatic  exception you know it uh  um and whereas the world of atoms has  been uh has been much slower for  for something like 50 years and thiel  himself admits that it can be difficult  to precisely quantify the pace of  innovation and its effect on the broader  economy  he tends to focus on three areas of  stagnation in particular  the first is the speed of travel he  cites the fact that the concorde was  shut down in 2003  and the true speed of air transportation  has been dropping ever since due to  increased airport security  the second is in healthcare where costs  have been increasing  well ahead of inflation for decades with  only moderate increases to life  expectancy and health outcomes to show  for it  he often points to the war on cancer  that president nixon declared in 1971  and has yet to see any promising  developments  lastly he focuses a lot of attention on  education  similar to healthcare education costs  have risen significantly above inflation  while median real wages have stagnated  his focus on median real wage growth is  fascinating to me because it's one of  the most  unexplained phenomena in present day  america and it has an immense impact on  everyday life  as opposed to a future where we all work  less and are still better off each year  he draws a comparison to the red queen's  race in alice in wonderland  in this futile race everyone must run  faster and faster to stay in the same  place  these are interesting points because  while teal often describes himself as a  libertarian  many left-leaning political commentators  agree with him that these are underlying  problems that need to be addressed they  just disagree on how best to remedy  these issues  teal points to two key areas that could  be possible solutions to the proble